  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Maximum stress <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Maximum</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>weight</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Cross</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>sectional</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>area</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>Now, maximum weight of steel wire</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo></math> Volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#215;</mo></math> Density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#215;</mo><mi>g</mi></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>g</mi></math></p><p>where, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi></math> is the maximum length of steel wire that can hang vertically without breaking. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi></math> is the density of steel and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> is the cross-sectional area of steel wire.<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo></math> Maximum stress, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>A</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>g</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p>",
